WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.200
In the 1950s, one of every two cars sold&nbsp;
in the U.S. came from General Motors.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.160
Those were the glory days.
GM’s market share declined in

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.360
the eighties and nineties and then the&nbsp;
company had to be bailed out by the US

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.720
government in the financial crisis of 2009.
Out of the ashes emerged a leaner company

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.280
led by Mary Barra, the first woman&nbsp;
to be CEO of a major automaker.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.400
Now, the home of Chevrolet, Cadillac, GMC,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.760
and Buick faces another test of&nbsp;
survival: the age of electric.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.760
Tesla is by far the undisputed&nbsp;
leader in electric vehicles.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.040
It delivered a record 309,000 EVs&nbsp;
in the fourth quarter of 2021.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.760
More than GM makes in an entire year!
Despite Tesla’s dominance,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.040
President Joe Biden praised GM’s CEO for&nbsp;
leading the world in electric vehicles.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.560
You did, Mary. You electrified the&nbsp;
entire automobile industry. I’m serious.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.120
You led. And it matters.
The numbers tell a different story.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:06.000
General Motors only sold 26 electric vehicles&nbsp;
in the U.S. in the fourth quarter of 2021.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.200
25 Chevy Bolts or the larger Bolt&nbsp;
EUVs and one electric Hummer pick up.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.520
Tesla CEO Elon Musk chimed in&nbsp;
there’s “room for improvement”.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:21.200
The reason for the abysmal numbers? GM halted&nbsp;
sales of its Bolts following a massive recall

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.560
due to the risk of batteries&nbsp;
spontaneously catching fire.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.000
2021 was rough for GM.
Its fourth-quarter sales fell 43%

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.160
compared to a year earlier, to 441,000 vehicles.
The company blamed the global shortage of

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.160
computer chips due to the pandemic.
Tesla on the other hand managed

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.480
the chip shortage by rewriting its&nbsp;
software to support alternative chips.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.440
Tesla’s EV market share in&nbsp;
the U.S. is a commanding 66%.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.120
GM’s is around 10%.
Despite Tesla’s massive EV lead, GM believes

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:02.240
it can top Tesla in sales as Barra told CNBC.
Do you think that you can catch Tesla by 2025?

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.280
Absolutely. When people get into these vehicles,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.720
they are just wowed. So we will be rolling them&nbsp;
out and we’re going to just keep working until

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.400
we have No. 1 market share in EVs.
General Motors is aiming for more

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.240
than 1 million global sales of EVs by&nbsp;
2025 which Tesla has already achieved.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:26.240
And it’s looking to grow even faster.
You know our goal as a company here is to grow

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.360
on an average pace of 50% per year.
A 50% growth rate per year based on current

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:37.760
figures would have Tesla easily surpass its target&nbsp;
of 20 million cars a year by 2030 - bolstered by

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.200
two additional gigafactories that will soon&nbsp;
come online: in Austin, Texas and Berlin.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.360
Meanwhile, GM is retooling half of&nbsp;
its manufacturing plants in the U.S.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.480
and China to make them capable of producing&nbsp;
electric vehicles by the end of the decade.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:58.000
General Motors sells more electric cars in China&nbsp;
through its joint ventures than in the U.S.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.760
And, in one of the most ambitious moves in the&nbsp;
auto industry, GM says it will stop selling

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:10.800
gasoline-powered vehicles altogether by 2035.
That is a complete U-turn by a company that

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:16.720
famously killed its electric car, the EV1, in&nbsp;
the nineties by crushing them in a junkyard - a

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.880
twist of fate that led to Tesla.
Two engineers disgusted by GM’s

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.160
actions founded Tesla Motors in 2003.
An all-electric future is hard to imagine

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.960
for a company that built its empire on fossil 
fuel powered vehicles as Morgan

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.440
Stanley analyst Adam Jonas pointed&nbsp;
out in his interview with GM’s CEO.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:42.240
But Mary, how do you exit a business that today&nbsp;
accounts or basically 100% of your profit and

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.600
cash flow? How do you do that? Well, we’ve got&nbsp;
14 years. And when you look at the fact that

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:54.800
we’ll have more than 30 vehicles that are EV&nbsp;
by 2025. We have aggressive plans after that.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:59.200
But time may not be on GM’s side.
Legacy car companies have a tricky

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.600
balancing act as they phase out their&nbsp;
old business to make way for the new.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.280
They have to figure out how to keep&nbsp;
their car dealerships happy, for example.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.760
Electric vehicles require less maintenance&nbsp;
as they have fewer moving parts which could

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.560
result in a significant loss of revenue for&nbsp;
a dealer. They don’t require oil changes and

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.200
routine repairs that make up as much&nbsp;
as half a dealership’s gross profits.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.960
And GM still has to figure out&nbsp;
how to make EVs profitable.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:31.040
Batteries are the priciest part of an electric&nbsp;
car. GM says its new Ultium batteries will drive

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:36.720
costs below $100 per kilowatt-hour - the magic&nbsp;
number that would make electric cars roughly

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.280
the same price as combustion engine ones.
Tesla hasn’t said what its battery price

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.640
target is - but its 4680 battery cells are&nbsp;
considered pivotal in helping the company

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:53.840
reach its goal of selling a $25,000 Tesla one day.
There’s a famous old saying about General Motors:

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.400
“As goes GM, so goes the Nation”
Implying that the company was so

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.440
powerful at one time, that the country’s&nbsp;
economic fortunes were tied to GM’s health.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.800
That’s clearly no longer the case.
Tesla’s market cap of $1 trillion

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.960
is 12 times higher than GM’s.
What was once the world’s largest

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:18.720
automaker is now the underdog, trying&nbsp;
to chip away at Tesla’s EV dominance.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.240
Electric cars are clearly the way of the future.
